venus 0114: a non-Apple-named IOKit accelerator service + an fps counter

Two independent things in one patch, because they are measured together:
the service that tells the userland (and us) what the GPU is, and the
frame rate counter that tells us how fast the CPU renderer actually runs.

PART 1 - SweetGraphicsAccelerator (iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetGPU.cpp, new)

The iOS 16.7 userland looks for a graphics accelerator all the time:
backboardd does IOServiceMatching("IOAcceleratorES") 5449 times and
IOServiceMatching("AGXAccelerator") 34 times per log and finds nothing
(logs/research/18-iokit-gap-inventory.md 2.1).  That is NOT a defect: with
no accelerator published, CoreAnimation falls back to its software
rasteriser and the UI works (upstream sweet renders at 20-30 fps with no
GPU patch at all).

The trap (logs/research/07-gpu-plan.md 4.3): if a service whose CLASS NAME
were "IOAcceleratorES" or "AGXAccelerator" were published, backboardd would
find it, CoreAnimation would pick the Metal renderer, and the closed
AGXMetalPlugin in the dyld shared cache would block waiting for a command
queue that does not exist - the whole UI freezes.  The rule of this project
is therefore: NEVER publish a class named IOAccelerator* or AGXAccelerator.

This service is the safe alternative:

  * the class is "SweetGraphicsAccelerator" (and its user client
    "SweetGraphicsAcceleratorUserClient").  IOServiceMatching compares the
    class name of a published service with the "IOProviderClass"/"IOClass"
    key of the matching dictionary, so this service can never answer a
    query for "IOAcceleratorES", "IOAcceleratorESLegacy", "AGXAccelerator"
    or "IOAccelerator".  It also cannot be picked up by a name match on
    "Accelerator" - the comparison is exact.
  * NONE of the properties that the Metal / CoreAnimation path keys on are
    published.  The full list with the reason for every omission is in the
    comment above sweet_gpu_publish() in the new file.  The important
    ones: no "IOGPUFamily" / "AppleGPUFamilyN" (the family selects the
    command stream format and the Metal plug-in), no "MetalPluginName" /
    "AGXMetalPlugin" / "IOGPUCommandQueue", no "IOAccelerator*" or "AGX*"
    or "IOGPU*" key, no "HIDServiceSupport" (the HID event system would
    subscribe to us), no "backlight-control" and no
    "IOSurfaceAcceleratorCapabilitiesDict" (those belong to other
    services).
  * what it does publish: IOUserClientClass, IOMaintainedDevice (a power
    management bookkeeping flag the kernel never reads), the version
    string, the device name, the chip id (0x06060001 = Adreno 660, taken
    from /arm-io/gpu when that node exists, otherwise from the CAF
    documentation), the pixel format list and the maximum texture size.
  * the user client answers the "device info" call (selector 0) with one
    structure - version string, pixel format list, max texture size - and
    three more selectors for the parts on their own.  Everything is
    answered from memory, so nothing can block: a framework that opens the
    client gets an answer instead of a hang.
  * boot-arg sweetgpuiokit=1 publishes the service.  Default OFF: the
    class is instantiated (the personality in KernelConfigTables.cpp
    attaches it to IOResources) but never published, so the registry looks
    exactly as it does today and the two behaviours can be compared on the
    same kernel build.

This is NOT a GPU driver: no command stream, no ringbuffer, no register
access, and the Adreno is not powered up.  The CX rail vote, the GPU_CC
clocks and the zap shader are stage B of report 07 and do not exist yet.

PART 2 - the fps counter (iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp)

Stage A1 of report 07 asks for a frame rate number in the log; today
there is none, so the CPU renderer cannot be compared with anything.
Boot-arg sweetfbfps=1 (default OFF) prints one line every 5 s:

  SWEET FB: fps=27 (swaps 138, presents 50, vsync 300, 5.020 s)

Three rates, because they answer different questions:
  fps      - the frames backboardd handed us per second (SwapEnd): the
             frame rate of the CoreAnimation CPU rasteriser, i.e. of the
             UI.  This is the number "fps=" is.
  presents - the frames actually copied into the panel per second; capped
             by sweetfb_hz (10 Hz by default), so it says how fast the
             screen changes, not how fast the UI is drawn.
  vsync    - the vsync timer ticks per second (60 Hz nominal): a sanity
             check on the timer itself.

The counters are taken under the swap lock, the report is made from the
vsync handler, and the time base is clock_get_uptime() +
absolutetime_to_nanoseconds() (no floating point in the kernel).

